# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Compression layer: Section 5 part B (5.6 - 5.13)

REVS = {
    # 5.6
    "P222": ("Posthumanism adds four things to the justice literature.",
             "Posthumanism adds four things to the justice literature. It extends the moral community beyond humans, so that materials, ecologies and future generations are participants in the arrangement rather than resources; it reframes justice as a property of composition as well as distribution \u2014 which actors and materials are admitted to the arrangement in the first place; it makes visible exclusions that human-centred recognition analysis misses, such as a dwelling that cannot accommodate a technology or infrastructure that excludes by design; and it supplies a critique of the technocratic view from nowhere, whose neutrality usually marks a position others do not hold."),
    "P223": ("Posthumanism also has limits, and they should be stated plainly.",
             "Posthumanism also has limits, stated plainly. It does not specify distributive criteria, generate policy instruments or offer a theory of institutions, and it can be vague about how decisions are actually made. Applied without a justice vocabulary it can dissolve human suffering into ontology: a woman who cannot afford paraffin is not a \u201cphenomenon\u201d; she is a person in a specific relation of deprivation, and the analysis must be able to say so."),
    "P224": ("Energy justice supplies the missing operational content.",
             "Energy justice supplies the missing operational content. The four dimensions used here \u2014 distribution, procedure, recognition and restoration \u2014 provide assessable criteria: who receives the service and who bears the cost; who participates and on what terms; whose knowledge and practices count; what past harm is repaired and what future capacity is built. They are not derived from posthumanism but from the justice literature (McCauley et al., 2013; Jenkins et al., 2016; Heffron and McCauley, 2017), and they are retained as a normative-operational complement."),
    "P225": ("Justice also has a scalar dimension.",
             "Justice also has a scalar dimension. Fraser (2009) argues that the frame \u2014 who is included in the \u201cwho\u201d of justice \u2014 is itself a question of justice. In an energy project the frame may be the household, the street, the settlement, the municipality or the planet, and each frame makes some claims visible and others invisible: a project that is just at settlement scale may be unjust regionally if it displaces pollution or extracts water. The study does not resolve this problem, but names it and builds a scalar check into the evaluation."),
    "P226": ("The combination is therefore deliberate.",
             "The combination is deliberate: posthumanism provides the ontology and the critique of technocracy, energy justice the criteria and the policy vocabulary, systems thinking the tools for mapping relations and feedback. None is sufficient alone, and the framework is designed so that each can be held accountable by the others."),
    "P227": ("One further point is necessary.",
             "One further point is necessary. The just transition literature has been criticised for treating \u201cthe community\u201d as a homogeneous beneficiary, and the criticism applies to a posthumanist framework too. Settlements are stratified by tenure, income, gender, age and political affiliation; a project that benefits \u201cthe community\u201d may benefit one group and burden another. The framework therefore requires disaggregated evidence and does not assume that a community-level outcome is a household-level outcome."),
    # 5.7
    "P230": ("User-centred design begins from the needs and experience of the user.",
             "User-centred design begins from the needs and experience of the user; participatory design involves users in the process; co-creation and co-research extend participation to generating ideas and producing knowledge (Sanders and Stappers, 2008; von Hippel, 2005). These traditions have improved design practice and given users a voice they did not have before."),
    "P231": ("They also carry humanist assumptions.",
             "But they carry humanist assumptions: the user as a centre of intention, the artefact as a tool, participation as extracting knowledge from users and transferring it into a design. The designer decides what counts as knowledge and what counts as a good design; the user is consulted, but the frame of the consultation is set elsewhere."),
    "P232": ("These assumptions are not wrong in every context,",
             "The assumptions are inadequate for a technology whose performance depends on the arrangement around it. If the problem is the water supply, the tenure arrangement or the maintenance contract, consulting users about stove preferences will not solve it. Participation has to extend to the elements the design tradition treats as context."),
    "P234": ("A posthumanist reconstruction treats the end-user",
             "A posthumanist reconstruction treats the end-user as a position within an assemblage rather than a centre of intention: the person whose practices, body, dwelling and relationships are enrolled in the technology. Their agency is real but not the only agency at work \u2014 the artefact, the fuel, the infrastructure and the institution also act. This does not diminish the user; it makes the user's knowledge more important, because the person who lives in the relation has knowledge the designer does not: situated, embodied, practical, not always articulate or consistent, but indispensable."),
    "P238": ("Tacit knowledge is embedded in practice and difficult to articulate:",
             "Tacit knowledge is embedded in practice and hard to articulate: a cook knows when a pot is right by sound and feel; a household knows how much paraffin to buy without calculating it. Situated knowledge is produced from a position (Haraway, 1988): a tenant knows the landlord's rules as an owner does not, and a woman knows the risks of cooking in a shared kitchen in ways a male researcher may not. Embodied knowledge is held in the body \u2014 the effort of carrying water, the smoke in the eyes \u2014 and cannot be reduced to information (Hayles, 1999)."),
    "P241": ("Practice-based knowledge is knowledge of how a task is actually done,",
             "Practice-based knowledge is how a task is actually done \u2014 workarounds, repairs, substitutions, informal rules \u2014 as distinct from how it is supposed to be done. Cultural knowledge is what a technology means: the appropriateness of a fuel for a dish, the symbolism of a flame, the norms governing who cooks and when. A design process that recognises these types will elicit them differently: interviews for articulate knowledge; observation and transects for tacit and practice-based knowledge; diaries and photovoice for embodied and situated knowledge; and workshops for cultural knowledge, if organised to allow disagreement."),
    "P245": ("Treating culture as a barrier to acceptance is misleading,",
             "Treating culture as a barrier to acceptance is misleading, and this study does not do so. A cultural practice is part of the standard against which a technology is judged: if a dish requires a visible flame, a technology with an invisible flame is inadequate for that dish. The question is not how to overcome culture but how to design a technology that can be enrolled in it, or how to negotiate a change in practice the household considers acceptable."),
    "P246": ("This does not mean that all practices should be accepted uncritically.",
             "This does not mean accepting all practices uncritically. The analysis must distinguish a preference from a constraint and from a compromise: a household using paraffin because electricity is unaffordable is managing a constraint, not expressing a cultural preference. The design response differs in each case."),
    "P248": ("Participation is not automatically good,",
             "Participation is not automatically good, and a framework that ignores its failure modes will romanticise the community it studies. Tokenism means residents are invited but unheard, their presence legitimising a decision already made (Arnstein, 1969). Elite capture lets the already-advantaged dominate the process and direct the benefits to themselves. Expectation inflation breaks trust when promises exceed delivery."),
    "P250": ("Consultation fatigue arises when communities are asked to participate repeatedly",
             "Consultation fatigue sets in when communities are asked repeatedly to participate in projects that do not endure, and the cost falls on those with the least time. Framing power means the agenda and vocabulary are set by the project, so residents can respond within the frame but not change it. The labour of participation is counted in meetings attended, not in effort contributed, and is often borne by women. Exit means the project ends and the arrangements built around it collapse. A posthumanist analysis adds material exclusion: a resident willing to participate may be unable to attend because the meeting is at a time she is cooking, in a place she cannot reach, or in a language she does not speak \u2014 a barrier that is material and institutional, and can be designed against."),
    "P259": ("The definition has four implications.",
             "The definition has four implications. Acceptance is observable in practice, not only in stated intention: a household that says it likes the system but does not use it has not accepted it. It is provisional, lost when a part fails or a price rises. It is distributed, depending on the maintenance arrangement, the fuel supply and the institution as much as on the household. And it is not the same as justice: a technology can be accepted because the household has no alternative."),
    # 5.8
    "P262": ("Section 4.4 introduced the Living Lab as a real-world arrangement for co-creation,",
             "Section 4.4 introduced the Living Lab as a real-world arrangement for co-creation, differentiated by who drives it and whether it is primarily a space, a method or a network (Bergvall-K\u00e5reborn and St\u00e5hlbr\u00f6st, 2009; Leminen et al., 2012; Mbatha and Musango, 2022). The question here is what would make such a lab systemic rather than systemic in name."),
    "P263": ("Systems thinking adds three resources.",
             "Systems thinking adds three resources. Boundary critique: Checkland (1981) argues that a system's boundary is a choice and Midgley (2000) that the choice has political consequences, determining whose interests are considered. Feedback: Meadows (2008) shows that system behaviour is produced by loops, delays and stocks, not by the intentions of its members. Emergence: Cilliers (1998) argues that complex systems have properties not predictable from their components, and that every model is a reduction."),
    "P264": ("A Living Lab becomes systemic, in this study, when three things are true.",
             "A Living Lab becomes systemic, in this study, when three things are true. Its boundary is chosen through explicit critique rather than convention, and what is excluded is recorded. Its feedback architecture is designed: the lab collects data on the effects of its own interventions and uses them to revise them. And its evaluation criteria derive from the system's purpose rather than the project's outputs: it asks whether the arrangement became more just and more secure, not only whether the prototype worked."),
    "P265": ("There is a further, distinctly posthumanist point.",
             "There is a further, distinctly posthumanist point. A Living Lab is an apparatus in Barad's sense: it does not observe a phenomenon from outside but participates in producing it. The questions asked, the instruments used, the people included and the data recorded all enact a cut, making some things visible and others invisible. Reflexivity about the cut is a condition of validity, not an optional virtue: a lab that presents its findings as neutral observations has misunderstood its own role."),
    "P266": ("This has practical consequences.",
             "The practical consequences follow. The lab documents its own decisions \u2014 why this site, these participants, this technology, these indicators. Dissenting views are recorded, not smoothed into consensus. The lab is accountable to the people it studies as well as to the funder. And the evaluation asks what the lab itself changed, including unintended changes."),
    "P267": ("From this argument the study derives six design principles",
             "From this argument the study derives six design principles for a systemic Living Lab, stated here and operationalised in Section 6."),
    # 5.10
    "P288": ("For RQ1, the theory supplies an account of what a system is,",
             "For RQ1, the theory supplies an account of what a system is, who decides its boundary, and how inequality is reproduced through the relations the boundary includes or excludes \u2014 without which the question could be answered by a descriptive taxonomy of lab types. For RQ2, it explains why participation is not simply a matter of inviting people: participation is an effect of the relations that make it possible \u2014 time, transport, language, care responsibilities, tenure, trust, authority \u2014 and the theory directs attention to the material arrangements that sustain or undermine those relations."),
    "P290": ("For RQ3, the theory explains why acceptance cannot be measured by asking people",
             "For RQ3, the theory explains why acceptance cannot be measured by asking whether people like a technology: acceptance is produced in the relation between artefact, fuel, dwelling, practice and institution, so the study observes use over time and traces the points at which it changes. For RQ4, it explains why a mechanism that works in one configuration may fail in another, directing design towards the maintenance arrangement, the fuel supply, the tariff, the governance rule and the siting decision, and insisting that a mechanism is not just unless it distributes benefits and burdens fairly. For RQ5, it provides the evaluation criteria: the lab is assessed not only on whether the prototype worked but on whether it changed the configuration in ways that increased justice and security."),
    # 5.11
    "P299": ("Empirical tractability. Posthumanism is often criticised for being abstract,",
             "Empirical tractability. Posthumanism is often criticised as abstract, and the study addresses the criticism by operationalising the concepts as observable relations: intra-action becomes the relation between artefact, household and infrastructure; distributed agency the set of actors that must be enrolled for the system to work; situated knowledge the knowledge residents hold and specific methods elicit. The test of the framework is whether it can be used in the field, not whether it is elegant."),
    "P300": ("Institutional fit. The theory was introduced by the supervisor",
             "Institutional fit. The theory was introduced by the supervisor and is supported by the research programme in which the study is located \u2014 a reason to take it seriously, not a sufficient reason to adopt it. The argument of this section is that the theory is defensible on its merits; if it were not, the institutional fit would be a reason to negotiate rather than to proceed."),
    "P304": ("Social practice theory is close to this study",
             "Social practice theory is close to this study in its attention to routine, materiality and convention (Shove, 2003; Stephenson et al., 2010) and informs the analysis of cooking and fuel stacking; its limitation is that it explains persistence better than intervention. Actor-network theory is the closest alternative: it shares the commitment to symmetry between humans and non-humans and offers a rich vocabulary for tracing associations, but it is deliberately indifferent to normative questions, and this study needs a theory that can say something about justice as well as association."),
    "P306": ("The capability approach (Nussbaum, 2011) provides a normative framework",
             "The capability approach (Nussbaum, 2011) provides a normative framework for wellbeing and freedom, compatible with this study's justice dimension, but it does not, by itself, account for technology or material agency, so it is a possible complement rather than a foundation. Political ecology and institutional theory explain the political and organisational conditions of energy transitions and inform the analysis of municipal capacity, but they operate at a different level from the design of a local arrangement. Systems thinking alone was rejected because it can describe a configuration without asking whether it is just; it is retained as a methodological complement."),
    # 5.13
    "P323": ("This section has argued that energy interventions in informal settlements",
             "This section has argued that energy interventions in informal settlements fail for theoretical as well as technical reasons. Dominant models treat acceptance as an individual decision about a finished artefact, and in doing so exclude the relations that determine whether a technology works. Critical posthumanism supplies the alternative ontology \u2014 entities emerging through relations, agency distributed, matter generative, knowledge situated \u2014 complemented by energy justice for normative criteria, systems thinking for mapping relations and feedback, and Living Lab theory for organising collaboration."),
    "P324": ("The framework has limits:",
             "The framework has limits: it does not specify distributive criteria, does not produce policy instruments, and can be difficult to operationalise. The study manages these limits by treating posthumanism as the foundation rather than the whole, energy justice as the operational complement, and by stating its claims in examinable form. The framework yields six propositions about acceptance, agency, boundary, apparatus, justice and participation, which Section 6 converts into components, relationships and indicators. The division of labour is deliberate: Section 5 explains why the study thinks the way it does; Section 6 shows what it will look at."),
}
